 malignancies there are now five fda  approved car t cell therapies four of  those are targeting the cd19 protein on  different b-cell cancers and then the  newest  is targeting the protein bcma  also on hematological malignancies and  this original fda approval was based on  really dramatic results with patients  who had  who had failed standard therapy  who normally have only a seven percent  five year survival rate with car t cell  therapy these patients now had over 83  percent complete responses  so in the clinic  the first two car t cell therapies that  were approved were kim ryan and yes  carter again they had really  exciting results because as you can see  here this is these are survival plots  of progression-free survival  over a time and you can see that even at  24 months post therapy about half the  patients are completely cancer free  and again this is much better than  traditional therapy however  there are still 60 percent of patients  or so who are not responding and so what  my lab is working on is trying to  address this 60  of patients who are not responding  and so what are the reasons for that  lack of response  so there are several uh limitations that  have now been discovered in terms of why  the therapy is not working for those  patients  and so  one of the original ones is that carb  failure can happen  at the product manufacturing phase so  patients can have t cells that won't  expand enough or they will have limited  persistence in the patients  another mechanism is having antigen  modulation so the patient's cancers can  actually lose the target lose the  targeted antigen either cd19 or cd20  and  or it could get um down modulated on the  surface so that car t cells are no  longer able to target those cancers  and then next there can also be  toxicities from the therapy being so  potent that will actually lead to  cytokine  responses that become toxic to other  tissues  and then finally there's a great unmet  need in treating other types of cancer  so currently these are working for  leukemias but solid tumors really make  the majority of the cancer  burden  around the world  and so to address these um these  shortcomings mylab and others are  working on developing universal car  t-cells and so what is a universal car  instead of it it's a car t cell where  instead of binding directly to a tumor  antigen the car  t cells are co-administered with these  tumor  targeting adapters or two anti-tumor  antibodies  and  the way the car works is that instead of  binding directly the tumor it binds to  the tag on the antibody and then the  antibody binds to the tumor cell and so  why do this  so this therapy potentially has several  advantages over current therapy  one of these is that there can be  tunable activity so instead of dosing in  different numbers of t cells what we can  do is we can dose in different amounts  of the  adapter that anti-tumor tagged antibody  and in dosing it more slowly you could  potentially avoid toxicity  from  by ramping up the amount of the  tumor activity anti-tumor activity in  the patients  this also allows the clinician to target  multiple different antigens or proteins  on the same tumor cell by applying  different anti uh tumor antibodies with  the universal car t cells  as you can see here in this diagram  and then finally there's it's also very  exciting because this universal car t  cell could be used not only for  hematological malignancies but also for  solid tumors  simply by combining the universal car t  cells with different anti-tumor adapters  and so it's a highly versatile therapy  compared to traditional therapy  and so again this is how the therapy  would work  so the patient um the patient's blood  cells are isolated and then transduced  with a virus to make the universal car  those cells are then expanded and then  reinfused back into the patient  and then at the same time  um the patient is administered with that  anti-tumor adapter molecule and this  again gives control over um  more control over the the timing of the  therapy as well as the potency as uh the  anti-tumor activity  and  to date um  there have been several anti uh  anti-tumor universal cartes cell  therapies developed and the different  variations really come in the type of  adapter that's being used with a therapy  as well as the targeting molecule on the  car so you can see here they fall into  four generic categories and i'm going to  tell you about a fifth one that we're  developing  in our lab so this first one is taking a  an antibody that has a natural binding  partner on the immune receptor and so  in this case the tag protein is  recognizing a biotin molecule that's  attached to the antibody adapter and  then that antibody recognizes the  antigen on the tumor cell  then there are also anti-tag cars which  recognize other tag molecules placed on  the adapter molecule  as well as uh by specific  engaging immune receptors and then adcc  mediating immune receptors which target  the constant region on the antibody  adapter molecule  and so what we're working on my in on in  my lab is developing universal snap car  t cells and so these are slightly  different from those uh other adapter  cards in uh that i went over on the last  page and so the way this works is our  car has as the targeting molecule  uh the protein snap  and this is a human enzyme that's  engineered to react with benzyl guanine  it's a benzyl guanine tag that goes on  the adapter molecule and what makes this  really unique is that instead of simply  binding to the tag the way that snap  works is it will  actually perform a chemical reaction  with the adapter and form a covalent  bond with the antibody adapter  and so when the antibody is administered  um it will form this covalent  interaction and then it can sense the  antigen and lead to t cell activation  and effector functions  and so  what we do in the lab is we conjugate  the  benzyl guanine  tag to the antibody and we've