r t cells in adapters and so  in this case we assayed for two  different outputs we looked at um one  marker  for  t cell  anti-tumor activity which is cytotoxic  granular release the marker cd107a  becomes upregulated on the t cells  and again we see a pattern in which only  when we have the matched antibody  and antigen  combination  do we have upregulation of cd107a  and then we actually looked at lysis of  tumor cells  and here  again we only see significant lysis of  the cells when we have  matched antigen  and antibody  tumor targeting specificity  okay and then  to  really show off the in  examine the  um  universality of the therapy we wanted to  see could we use the therapy to actually  at the same time  target multiple antigens so this would  be what we'd want to do to try to avoid  um cancer relapse due to antigen loss so  if a tumor has both for instance cd20  and egfr can we use both antibodies and  efficiently target  the  both antigens at the same time to avoid  that loss  or if we had a tumor with pre-existing  variation where some cells only had cd20  and some had only egfr could we  eliminate  all of those cells in addition to the  ones that have both  and so what we did was we mixed or we we  first again we're looking at specific  lysis so this is killing of  um  cd20 tumor cells using the rituximab  with bg as well as egfr expressing cells  that have  killing of those using a cytoxama with  bg we see very efficient and specific  killing  using those antibodies on the single  cell populations and then when we mix  the cells alone what we see is this on  the top plot we have the killing  of only the egfr cells and so when we  add in cytosumab alone we see efficient  killing of those cells but not of the  cd20 positive cells on the bottom plot  and similarly when we  add in rituximab which targets cd20 we  only see significant lysis of the cd20  positive rawgi cells  but not of the egfr cells and when we  combine both antibodies we see very  very efficient killing of both target  cell populations so in indeed this could  be a good therapy to target multiple  antigens at the same time  and so for next steps we're moving the  therapy uh we're advancing our  development of the therapy to testing in  animal models and i'm not going to go  over those experiments today but i do  want to briefly cover the  the current  status of universal car t cell therapies  as they're moving to the clinic so now  as i mentioned there are several groups  who are developing  universal car therapies and they are  moving to the clinic and here you can  see  um  several of the different technologies  and their current status and i want to  include this  also as just a general reference  but there are several  trials being undertaken targeting  different blood cancers as well as now  some solid tumors um using trastuzumab  targeting her too  and so these are very exciting and  we hope to  advance the snapcar t-cell therapy also  to trials in the near future  so in general  there are several  pros to  car t cell therapy but there are also  some limitations and i just wanted to  review those here so the pros are that  universal cards can target multiple  antigens in the same tumor in order to  potentially prevent relapse because you  can combine the universal car t cells  with multiple adapters to target  different antigens at the same time and  so if if the cells lost cd19 you could  also be targeting cd20 at the same time  for instance  you could also use the same car t cells  for both a  hematological malignancy as well as a  solid tumor just by combining them with  different adapters and finally um we can  also control activity by dosing in  different amounts of the adapter  and so for the cons  so it is a more complex therapy so of  course  the therapy now consists of both the  cell component and some other adapter  component so that could be more  complicated for regulatory reasons um  but in general um  as we're using already approved  antibodies i think that's less of a  concern there's also some concern with  tumor penetration using adapters um  because you also need the adapters to  potentially penetrate the tumor as well  as the t cells and we're hoping with the  snapcar t cells as they're covalently  labeling the t cells that um this would  be less of an issue because um the tumor  trafficking of the t cells  the t cells can be pre-labeled by the  antibody and then traffic to the tumor  okay and so now i'm going to switch  gears into some next directions that we  are undertaking with universal car  t-cell therapy  and so  the first is  has to do with a new variety of  engineered receptor uh  this is work out of uh wendell lim's lab  and ucsf as well as cold royal in  developing these syn notch receptors and  so a traditional car  upon binding to an antigen will activate  t cell effector functions um like  cytokines interferon gamma and target  cell killing but the way that syn notch  receptors work is that they will sense  an antigen and then activate any gene or  genes of interest in response so it's a  much more programmable kind of designer  gene response that they can make  and so the cool thing about this is that  you can engineer t cells that would  locally deliver  a specific payload instead of simply  focusing on killing the tumor cells  maybe  a better idea is to have them  help out  other things such as  well different other therapeutic agents  which could either have anti-tumor  function or could be used for other  types of diseases  as well as controlling the fate of the t  cell  and also stimulating endogenous immunity  so can you make um some molecules that  will activate other immune cells to have  uh anti-disease function  and so what we wanted to do uh was  uh could we see if we could make uh  t cells that had universal syn notch  receptors to provide some of the  benefits of the universal cars to the  syn notch receptor technology  and  so again this is the way the synod  receptor works  so it takes a novel environmental input  and leads to a novel transcriptional  output and so what happens is upon  binding to  the target antigen  the receptor will get pulled and then  cleaved and released a transcription  factor to turn on the designer gene  program and so what we wanted to do was  create one using the snap protein so we  made a  snap snin notch receptor that we would  combine with benzoyl guanine tagged  after the antibody adapters  and then lead to a covalently assembled  receptor  and then  downstream also in the same cell we have  a response gene uh which has a promoter  sensitive to the transcription factor  and then an output gene in this case  we're using  tag vfp blue fluorescent protein  and so upon antigen binding the receptor  will get cleaved leading to production  of that output gene  and so if indeed  when we add  these receptors to t cells  and we combine them with or without the  adapter on the left we have an anti-cd19  adapter and we uh have them either with  cd19 positive or cd19 negative cells and  we assay for tag dfp expression we see  that we are able to redirect um snap sin  notch activity  to produce the output bfp protein only  in response to antibody adapter and  antigen on the targeted cells  and we can also do this using an  anti-cd20 adapter  the rituximab adapter again seeing only  response when we have  um  the cd20 positive cell  so this is very exciting and now we're  working on uh applying this technology  to lead to production of therapeutic  gene outputs to try to enhance car t  cell therapy using these engineered  synaptic receptors  and so now i'm going to tell you about  another new direction that we're we're  looking at and this is  applying conditional control to the  universal car therapy and we're looking  at um both uh two different types of off  switch  control mechanisms and so one of these  is can we use light to actually  control the car t cell technology so can  we make a car t cell that is  controlled by light to actually avoid an  anatomical site in the body  and  so here you can see we have a brain  which  uh has a tumor on one part but maybe we  know that the car that we're going to be  using or the antibody has some  off-target activity on another region of  the brain so we need can we  create  control where we can shine light on that  part of the  organ and then protect it from car t  activity  and then also could we create a system  that maybe would have systemic toxicity  but that we could tune it or switch it  off if we see that toxicity in the  patient using a small molecule  and so  we have a collaboration with the dieters  lab in chemistry trying to develop this  and they're experts at creating uh  conditional chemistry control and we  have um  because we're adding this benzoyl  guanine  to the adapter we're already using  chemistry  this is actually a great venue to try  out these reactive chemistries to try to  get conditional control and so the way  this will work is we'll take our  traditional adapter but instead of being  connected having the benzyl guanine  connected by an inert linker it will  have a reactive linker that will either  get cleaved by light stimulation or by a  small molecule addition and so when the  adapter is present and it has um  no stimulus attached it will act like  any other adapter and activate uh snap  cartier cell activity but when light or  the small molecule are applied it will  cleave that benzyl green guanine off and  won't lead to any signaling by the  therapeutic t cells  okay and so um  working with the dieter's lab we created  these  cleavable offswitch adapter  where um  it's reactive to blue light so when the  blue light  is applied to the system it will cut off  the um the tag molecule on the adapter  and so we combined the  the off switch adapters with the  universal car t cells as well as the  target cells and then we applied light  for different amounts of time and you  and then acid for tumor cell lysis and  what you can see on the right  um is that if we had traditional car t  cells um in green and blue  if we um  applied  them to tumor cells they were unaffected  by light stimulus  and if we also used a an adapter that  had an inert peg 2 linker which is our  standard linker  in orange it also was not affected by  light but when we used our light  cleavable  adapters at two different concentrations  we saw that we get  induced  suppression of the  tumor cell lysis and so we were able to  con  turn off  lysis using the light for these two  adapter antibodies and in even at 10  seconds of light exposure uh we already  saw a dramatic reduction in near  complete and  reduction by 45 seconds of exposure  and so for the the clinic the real  excitement comes to applying this  where light is already being used which  is in fluorescence guided surgery so  currently  there are some systems which in which  patients are applied with a fluorescent  contrast agent and this helps to  visualize the tumors in the patients  um  and  help guide the surgeons who are who are  performing treatment and so um what  we're trying to do is to also create uh  cleavable or activatable linkers that  are reactive to um other wavelengths of  light  so in the near infrared wavelengths to  hopefully use them in this  type of uh therapy  so we also  built a drug controlled off switch and  in this case we're using a small  molecule drug  phosphine uh  called 2d pbm  and what we can see on the right is that  if we add the drug and the adapter at  different concentrations  to  um tumor cells and then we we stain for  adapter signaling on the z-axis you can  see we can get very tunable control of  the amount of tag on the surface of the  tumor cells  and then similarly when we go to  look for  control of car t cell  tumor cell killing we also see we get  very nice tunable control over car t  cell function  and you can see again if we have  inert linkers the peg two linkers  or a 